-- task-graph/hyperactivity.cub — this work's nodes (lib/task-graph.cub; the meaning and rules are docs/task-graph-design.md, read it
-- first). A node's definition gives its identity and the nodes it is a child of; nodes are defined
-- after their parents. A node's children always cover it completely; where they are not certainly
-- complete, one child is the node's residual. A goal's statement is the comment `name (date):` above
-- its definition. A node opened for a specific piece of work (named tN) has its text as the one
-- comment `tN (date):` at the place in the code it concerns (`python3 tools/task-graph.py show tN`), or here
-- below when it has no single place (residuals among them). Every node here is open: resolving one
-- removes it (and its text) and folds what matters into where it belongs; the history keeps it.
-- Loaded after lib/task-graph.cub.
--
-- t33 (2026-11-12): question. Not yet done: reading the evaluator's rule bodies (src/net.c's
-- interaction cases, src/dnf.c's face algebra) side by side with the published rules, CCHM (Cohen,
-- Coquand, Huber, Mörtberg, TYPES 2015, LIPIcs 69) and the clocked extension (Kristensen,
-- Møgelberg, Vezzosi, arXiv:2102.01969), each rule recorded as matching or deviating at its site.
-- The two papers are not in the repository; fetch them. Until this is done, conformance of the
-- implementation to the calculus is not known.
--
-- t34 (2026-11-12): question. Not yet done: reading src/lang.c's translation of terms into the
-- evaluator's graph for anything that can change a term's meaning (the strictness analysis that
-- decides which operands are built eagerly, fully lazy lifting, sweeping of compiled code, the
-- module cache, whose key is the build date and the files' texts and whose hits are trusted).
--
-- t35 (2026-11-12): question. Not yet done: reading lib/check.cub's typing clauses against the
-- calculus's typing rules, each recorded as matching or deviating at its site.
--
-- t74 (2026-11-13): residual of language-implementation: determine and do every essential (non-
-- example) construction of the paper, and every feature of the language, that its other children do
-- not cover.
--
-- t75 (2026-11-13): needs-work. The paper's worked examples as checked definitions (tracks/
-- PAPER-READING.md records each as absent): §5.1's intrinsically typed language and its
-- substitution theorem; §5.2's let elimination (5.4); §5.5's memory interpreter and its trace;
-- §5.6's cancel, cancelSound, preserve; (3.7) A ≃ Graph(f); §4's Vec ≃ (Fin → A) with Sorted
-- transported, Monoid, Package, graph relabeling; §6.3's semiring interpretations (6.11–6.13) and
-- Clauses/fold (6.14); §6.4's conservative extension (6.17–6.18); §9.3's stack machine (9.2–9.10),
-- the toy ISA trace (9.14) and the 46-byte decoder with χ_b; §8.3's encodeLE/decodeLE (8.6–8.12);
-- §10.3's credit conservation with receipts; §10.7's mixed-version invariant; Appendix A.
--
-- t76 (2026-11-13): residual of the-product: determine and do everything else the product requires
-- that the language implementation, the paper examples, the product implementation and language
-- support do not cover.
--
-- t79 (2026-11-13): residual of language-support: determine and do every language to be lifted
-- beyond C++, Rust and x86-64 (C, OCaml, Python and others).
--
-- t80 (2026-11-13): residual of c-plus-plus: determine and do the complete lifting of C++ (the whole
-- standard: every static and dynamic category of the paper's §8.6), beyond the specific tickets.
--
-- t81 (2026-11-13): residual of rust: determine and do the complete lifting of Rust, beyond the
-- specific tickets.
--
-- t83 (2026-11-13): residual of x86-64: determine and do the x86-64 model complete, every
-- instruction translated, beyond t31.
--
-- t84 (2026-11-13): residual of memory-models: determine and do x86-TSO and RC11 as exact types
-- with the standard mapping of C atomics to x86 proved correct, beyond t27.
--
-- t85 (2026-11-13): residual of linux-kernel-interface: determine and do the operating-system semantics (the kernel
-- interface, lib/os) as exact types, so a running program is a process whose system-call events are
-- its interaction.
--
-- t86 (2026-11-13): residual of exact-calculus: determine and do the implementation exactly the
-- published calculus: every rule present, none added, the trusted part small and plainly those
-- rules, everything else checked by it or unable to change a result, beyond the specific tickets.
--
-- t87 (2026-11-13): residual of typed-specializer: determine and do the specializer with its law
-- in its type, the three Futamura projections (6.6-6.7), and specialization at run time with the
-- residual checked, realized and cached with its evidence (paper §9.3), beyond the specific
-- tickets.
--
-- t88 (2026-11-13): residual of native-realization: determine and do realization through C keeping the
-- program's structure, the translation returning the relation between running the C and the term's
-- meaning, native code substituted only with that relation, a process as an executable, beyond the
-- specific tickets.
--
-- t89 (2026-11-13): residual of typed-checker: determine and do the checker returning a typing
-- derivation or a refusal, then shown to decide derivability (paper §6.1: check : Dec(Der)), beyond
-- the specific tickets.
--
-- t90 (2026-11-13): residual of typed-codes: determine and do typed codes Code(Γ, A) over the one
-- representation, every definition reflected to one, normalization with its soundness (6.5), beyond
-- the specific tickets. Known part of it (2026-11-13, from reading the paper): (6.6) needs an
-- interpreter whose source type is a syntax datatype and (6.7) needs lift_A on a syntax datatype,
-- while lib/nbe.cub's plan has only Bool, Π, Σ, PathP, Kan operations, Glue and universes as object
-- types: object-level inductive data types are missing from the plan.
--
-- t91 (2026-11-13): residual of product-implementation: determine and do everything else the live
-- interactive collaboration system and its tooling require, beyond the specific tickets
-- (docs/state-change-collaboration-design.md).
--
import "../lib/task-graph.cub"
-- the-product (2026-11-13, Avik): "the product is every screened device in the world simply runs
-- hyperactive kernel. the term "application" becomes fully concrete - does not refer to a silo, but
-- literally to a particular application of computing." "*all* of this is to serve *product*." The
-- root: what Avik wants (docs/what-hyperactive-is-for-in-avik-words.md).
the-product : Node
the-product = node "The product: every screened device in the world runs Hyperactive." nil
-- language-implementation (2026-11-13, Avik): "complete implementation of hyperactive programming
-- language & features as specified by the paper ... get to the point i can show someone the paper
-- and correctly state "i have implemented this fully correctly" (obviously, that doesn't include
-- constructions about random examples about trading systems or message protocols)". The
-- initial high-priority deliverable.
language-implementation : Node
language-implementation = node "The Hyperactive language and its features, implemented completely and correctly as the paper specifies." (cons the-product nil)
-- paper-examples (2026-11-13, Avik): "term-by-term construction of every single construction
-- including examples, appendix, in paper, as code in repo cited by content-address in paper ...
-- expressely de-prioritized ... while this *serves nothing*." It serves no node but the root, so
-- the order of work gives it nothing while anything else is open.
paper-examples : Node
paper-examples = node "Every construction in the paper, its examples and appendix included, as a term in the repository, cited from the paper by content address." (cons the-product nil)
-- product-implementation (2026-11-13, Avik): "all the product work of building a live interactive
-- collaboration system & tooling over the language & its terms". How it is held, changed and shared:
-- docs/state-change-collaboration-design.md.
product-implementation : Node
product-implementation = node "The live interactive collaboration system and tooling over the language and its terms." (cons the-product nil)
-- language-support (2026-11-13, Avik): "Languages support (semantics/syntax as terms for particular
-- languages) * RISC x86, C++, C, Rust, OCaml, Python etc". "before we start any such work, certain
-- things need to be at perfection": it waits on language-implementation and product-implementation
-- (after, below).
language-support : Node
language-support = node "The syntax and semantics of particular languages as terms: x86, C, C++, Rust, OCaml, Python and others." (cons the-product nil)
-- exact-calculus (2026-11-12): the implementation is exactly the published calculus, CCHM (Cohen,
-- Coquand, Huber, Mörtberg 2018) with clocked guarded computation (Kristensen, Møgelberg, Vezzosi
-- 2022): every rule present, none added, the trusted part small and plainly those rules, everything
-- else checked by it or unable by construction to change a result.
exact-calculus : Node
exact-calculus = node "The implementation is exactly the published calculus, with a minimal trusted part." (cons language-implementation nil)
-- typed-checker (2026-11-12): the checker returns a typing derivation or a refusal, so it is sound by
-- construction; then it is shown to decide derivability (paper §6.1: check : Dec(Der)).
typed-checker : Node
typed-checker = node "The checker returns a typing derivation or a refusal, and decides derivability (paper §6.1)." (cons exact-calculus nil)
-- typed-specializer (2026-11-12): the specializer with its law in its type (6.6), the first, second
-- and third Futamura projections (6.6-6.7), and specialization when values arrive at run time, the
-- residual checked, realized and cached with its evidence (paper §9.3).
typed-specializer : Node
typed-specializer = node "The specializer carries its law in its type, with the three Futamura projections (paper §6)." (cons language-implementation nil)
-- native-realization (2026-11-12): realization through C, emitting C that keeps the program's
-- structure; the translation returns the relation between running the C and the term's meaning; the
-- evaluator substitutes native code only with that relation (paper §9.3).
native-realization : Node
native-realization = node "Programs are realized as native code through C, with the relation to their meaning (paper §9)." (cons language-implementation nil)
-- typed-codes (2026-11-12): the language's own terms as typed objects in it, Code(Γ, A) = Σ c.
-- Der(Γ, c, A) (paper §6.1), over the one existing representation of terms; every definition
-- reflects to one; normalization as a term with its soundness equation (6.5).
typed-codes : Node
typed-codes = node "The language's terms are typed objects in it, with normalization and its soundness (paper §6.1)." (cons typed-specializer (cons native-realization (cons typed-checker nil)))
-- c-plus-plus (2026-11-12): the complete lifting of C++ at the source level: the whole semantics,
-- memory and threading, templates and all compile-time machinery as terms, the abstract machine
-- distinct from any implementation at given flags.
c-plus-plus : Node
c-plus-plus = node "C++: the whole standard as terms." (cons language-support nil)
-- rust (2026-11-12): the lifting of Rust, as for C++.
rust : Node
rust = node "Rust as terms." (cons language-support nil)
-- x86-64 (2026-11-12): the x86-64 instruction set's semantics complete (the imported model,
-- lib/isa/x86), every instruction translated.
x86-64 : Node
x86-64 = node "x86-64: every instruction's semantics as terms." (cons language-support nil)
-- memory-models (2026-11-12): memory consistency models as exact types: x86-TSO (lib/isa/tso.cub)
-- and the C/C++ model RC11 (lib/mem/rc11.cub), with the standard mapping of C atomics to x86 correct.
memory-models : Node
memory-models = node "The memory models x86-TSO and RC11 as terms, and the mapping between them." (cons c-plus-plus (cons x86-64 nil))
-- linux-kernel-interface (2026-11-12): the Linux kernel interface as exact types (lib/os), so a
-- running program is a process whose system-call events are its interaction.
linux-kernel-interface : Node
linux-kernel-interface = node "The Linux kernel interface as terms." (cons language-support nil)
t3 : Node
t3 = node "t3" (cons exact-calculus nil)
